## Agent skills

### Issue tracker

Issues are tracked as local markdown files under `.scratch/`. See `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`.

### Triage labels

Default label vocabulary. See `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.

### Domain docs

Single-context layout. See `docs/agents/domain.md`.

## Layout

Two implementations live side by side until the M6 cutover
(`.scratch/ts-rewrite/`, ADR-0004): the Go CLI (`cmd/roast`,
`internal/`) and its TypeScript rewrite (`src/`, `test/`, `scripts/`).
Both are under active change. `npm run test:go` runs the Go and launcher
tests; `npm test` runs typecheck and the TS suite; lint is
`npm run lint`.

## Area notes

Before editing code in an area, read its notes. Each area file holds the
Go and TS notes for that area side by side.

- Any Go code or Go test: `docs/agents/go.md`
- Any TS in `src/`, `test/`, or `scripts/`: `docs/agents/typescript.md`
- Config loading, roles, providers, the `init` starter,
  `roast.schema.json` (`internal/config`, `src/config/`):
  `docs/agents/config.md`
- Trust and approvals (`internal/trust`, `src/trust/`, `*/trust.*`):
  `docs/agents/trust.md`
- Stored credentials, `login`, `logout` (`internal/credentials`,
  `src/credentials/`, `internal/cli/*login*`): `docs/agents/auth.md`
- Gate and the pre-commit hook (`internal/gate`, `src/gate/`):
  `docs/agents/gate.md`
- Agent skill install (`internal/skill`, `src/skill/`):
  `docs/agents/skill.md`
- Run records (`internal/record`): `docs/agents/records.md`
- Scope, context, exploration tools, snapshot denylist (`src/scope/`,
  `src/context/`, `src/snapshot/`): `docs/agents/scope-context.md`
- LLM adapters and the agent loop (`src/llm/`): `docs/agents/llm.md`
- npm packaging, the launcher, release (`packages/`, `scripts/build.ts`,
  `src/launcher.ts`): `docs/agents/packaging.md`

A new note goes in its area's file; a new area gets a file and a line
here. This file holds only what most tasks hit.

## Traps every task hits

- `JSON.parse` syntax errors can quote the input. Credential-file parse
  errors must use value-free messages so diagnostic output cannot expose
  a stored key.
- Shell hooks block Bash commands that contain a heredoc mentioning
  `process.env.PATH`, a heredoc or command containing `env:` or `.env`
  text, or a `credentials*` path together with `cat`, `sed`, `head`, and
  the like. Read and write such files with the editor tools.
- Tests that write project state (run records, trust, stored
  credentials) chdir into a temporary Git repository first. `.roast/`
  ignores itself, so a leak into this checkout is invisible to
  `git status`; check with `ls -a`.
- CLI diagnostic tests that resolve roles need valid test credentials
  and local fake-server routing, even when asserting only local checks.
- `.npmrc` sets `force=true` so npm links workspaces whose `os`/`cpu`
  differ from the host. Expect "using --force" warnings from every npm
  command.
- The CLI parser preserves dashed option names and disables boolean
  negation. Declare flags such as `no-browser` explicitly and read
  `args['no-browser']`, rather than a camel-case property.
- `simulateTerminals` only bypasses interactive-terminal detection. To
  exercise raw-mode prompt input through the CLI, give stdin an `isTTY`
  value of `true` and a `setRawMode` function; assert raw mode is restored.

## Docs

- User-facing docs (`README.md`, `docs/`, the bundled
  `internal/skill/assets/roast/SKILL.md`) write every invocation as
  `npx roast ...`; Roast is a project dependency, never a `PATH` binary.
  Settings are documented as `.roast.json` keys, never environment
  variables; only credentials, `ROAST_SKIP`, and test-only `ROAST_LIVE_*`
  / `ROAST_TEST_*` variables exist.
- CLI messages in `internal/cli` (for example the gate hint printed
  during review) still say bare `roast`; changing them is a code change,
  not a docs change.
# Roast

An adversarial, cross-model code reviewer installed as a project dependency
via npm. One model proposes defects in a Git diff and a second model checks
each one before it reaches the report.

## Language

### Review

**Finder**:
The role whose model reads the diff and proposes candidate findings.
_Avoid_: reviewer, author model

**Verifier**:
The role whose model re-reads the cited code and confirms or refutes each
candidate finding.
_Avoid_: checker, judge

**Role**:
One of the two review positions, finder or verifier, each bound to one
inference profile.

**Inference profile**:
A named, complete unit of model, endpoint, protocol, output limit and
credential source.
_Avoid_: provider, backend

**Run record**:
The stored evidence of one review, kept inside the project that was reviewed.
_Avoid_: log, history

**Gate**:
The pre-commit hook that runs a quick review of staged content and fails open.
_Avoid_: pre-commit check, hook

### Configuration

**Project config**:
The `.roast.json` file (JSONC) nearest the working directory, searched upward
no further than the Git toplevel. It is the single home for every non-secret
setting.
_Avoid_: repo config, local config, rc file

**Trusted project config**:
A project config whose security-relevant contents (non-default endpoints,
credential variable names, prompt-override files) the user has explicitly
approved for this clone. Roast does not send code or credentials anywhere a
project config names until it is trusted.
_Avoid_: allowed config, whitelisted config

**Credential**:
An API key. Credentials are the only settings that come from outside the
project config: from environment variables, or from a stored credential. A
project config may name the variable but never hold the value.
_Avoid_: secret, token

**Built-in provider**:
A provider Roast knows by name (OpenAI, Anthropic, OpenRouter): its
endpoint, the protocols it accepts there, its standard key variable, and
how to log in.
A profile on a built-in provider's endpoint with its standard key variable
needs no trust approval.
_Avoid_: default provider, known provider

**Stored credential**:
A built-in provider's key kept in a credentials file, scoped to the project
(inside the Git directory) or to the user. It ranks below every environment
variable and is sent only to that provider's own endpoint (ADR-0002).
_Avoid_: saved key, cached token
# TypeScript rewrite

Applies to `src/`, `test/`, and `scripts/`. Notes for one area are in
that area's file (index in `AGENTS.md`).

## Tooling

- `npm test` runs `typecheck`, then bundles and runs the tapzero suite.
  A new test module must be imported from `test/index.ts`. esbuild drops
  unused imports without resolving them, so only `typecheck` catches a
  broken import.
- Lint with `npm run lint`: it preloads `scripts/eslint-ts6.ts`, which
  gives eslint the TypeScript 6 API because typescript-eslint throws on
  the TypeScript 7 that `typecheck` uses.
- Legacy launcher and release files are excluded by name from lint (the
  `--ignore-pattern` list) and typecheck (`tsconfig.json` `exclude`);
  the launcher compiles with `tsconfig.launcher.json`.
- Node runs `scripts/` directly (type stripping), so they use only
  erasable syntax: no enums, namespaces, or parameter properties.
- `baseOptions` in `scripts/esbuild-options.ts` holds the esbuild
  options every bundle shares (text loaders for `.tmpl`, `.md`, `.sh`;
  `mainFields: ['module', 'main']`, which `jsonc-parser`'s UMD build
  needs); `scripts/bundle.ts` adds the `require` shim banner. A test
  that bundles code itself uses both.
- `npm run bundle` also writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt`
  (`scripts/licenses.ts`) and fails, naming the package, when a bundled
  package lacks an allowlisted `license` field or a license file. A new
  runtime dependency must pass it; extending `ALLOWED_LICENSES` needs
  maintainer approval. `@substrate-system/debug` fails it today, so no
  `src/` module may import it yet.

## Source conventions

- Keep source ASCII: write `\uXXXX` escapes. Some editing tools decode
  escapes into literal characters when writing; check with
  `grep -nP '[^\x00-\x7f]'` after writing a file. eslint `--fix`
  (quote-props) rewrites a quoted `'\u00e9'` object key into a literal
  non-ASCII identifier; put such keys in a const and use a computed key.
- eslint rejects control characters in regex literals
  (`no-control-regex`) and labeled statements (`no-labels`); scan char
  codes and use helper functions instead.
- Startup guards live in `src/startup.ts`, not `src/cli.ts`: importing
  the entry runs the CLI, so testable helpers sit in other modules.
- Module-level swappable functions use a mutable hooks object plus a
  delegating export (`terminalHooks`, `skillHooks.lookPath`), because
  tests cannot reassign an ESM `let` export. Swap the object's field
  and restore it in `t.cleanup`.
- Shared helpers; import these, never re-implement:
  `src/gocompat/` (`strings.ts`: Go TrimSpace/Ext/`%.2f`/strconv.Quote/
  byte-order sort and `truncateUtf8`; `duration.ts`: Go durations as
  bigint nanoseconds; `match.ts`: Go `path.Match`),
  `src/textsafe/textsafe.ts`, `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`
  (`writeFileAtomic`, options cover trust, credentials, and gate temp
  names and error messages), `src/git/git.ts` (the only git runner;
  pass the CLI context's `env`, it reads `process.env` only when none is
  given), `src/util/pool.ts` (`mapPool`).

## CLI core (`src/cli/`)

- Commands are `CommandDef`s (`command.ts`) listed in `commands.ts`;
  `run.ts` builds the yargs tree from that list plus `registerCommand`
  (tests), and calls the handler only after parsing succeeds.
- A handler gets a `RunContext` (cwd, env, signal, fetch, one shared
  `LineReader`) and throws `UsageError` / `ExitSignal` / any error; it
  never writes the error itself or reads process globals.
- A group with `subcommands` and no handler prints its help.
- Terminal detection and browser opening are swapped through
  `terminalHooks` (`terminal.ts`).
- With only the default `$0` command registered, yargs omits the
  Commands section from root help; it appears once a second command
  registers.

## Tests

- Use the `test` wrapper in `test/helpers/test.ts`, which adds Go-style
  `t.cleanup` (tapzero has no teardown hook). tapzero's `Test` has no
  `notOk`; write `t.ok(!x, ...)`.
- CLI tests run in-process with `runRoast` (`test/helpers/run.ts`),
  which makes a temp repo with an isolated env (`test/helpers/repo.ts`:
  `tempRepo`, `tempDir`, which also sets `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`).
  `test/cli/process.ts` spawns `dist/cli.js`, so it needs a fresh
  `npm run bundle` (`npm test` does that).
- Swap terminals and the browser with `simulateTerminals` /
  `captureBrowser` (`test/helpers/terminal.ts`).
- Tests that spawn processes use `test/helpers/spawn.ts` (`ROOT`,
  `run`).
- Git runner tests observe a child by putting a fake `git` shell script
  first on the injected `PATH` (spawn resolves the command through
  `env.PATH`); see `test/git/git.ts`.
- Live tests live under `test/live/` (entry `index.ts`,
  `npm run test:live`); each passes with a "skipped" note when its
  `ROAST_LIVE_*` key is unset, so the TAP stream is never empty.

## Differential harness (`test/diff/`, deleted at M6)

- `npm run test:diff` runs the harness self-tests (no Go needed), then
  every scenario against the Go baseline built at `cd08f10` into
  `node_modules/.cache/roast-diff/` (delete it to force a rebuild).
  Without `go` on `PATH` every scenario is a TAP skip.
- Add a milestone's scenarios as a `Scenario[]` under
  `test/diff/scenarios/` and list it in `SCENARIOS` (`index.ts`);
  `server`, `records`, and `requests` turn on the fake server and
  record/request comparison.
- A difference caused by a deliberate change goes in `ALLOWLIST`
  (`allowlist.ts`) naming the change number. Go behavior judged wrong
  goes in the owning area's Open questions, never patched in the
  harness.
# LLM layer and agent loop (TS)

`src/llm/`.

## Adapters

- Adapters read no environment variable. A protocol is an entry in
  `ADAPTERS` (`client.ts`) with a pure encoder and an
  `AsyncIterable<SseEvent>` decoder returning a `ModelTurn`; `send` owns
  fetch, headers (`http.ts` `requestHeaders`), capture, usage, and
  abort.
- One `errors.ts`: `LlmError.kind` (underscore names, read by
  `src/cli/errors.ts`) and `sentinels` (hyphen names, `hasSentinel`)
  must stay in step.
- Tool results sent to models go through `encodeToolResult` (`json.ts`)
  only.

## Agent loop (`agent.ts`, `runConversation`)

- `createClient(modelClient, {ledger, now, sleep, sessionId})`
  (`client.ts`) wraps a `ModelClient` into the finder/verifier `Client`.
  The pure decision is `reviewResponse` (`review.ts`); the deadline,
  ledger, final-argument validation, and retries are separate modules.
- `ModelClient.send` owns the request timeout; the loop composes only
  caller + deadline/window signals.
- Cutoff errors come from `cutoffError` (`errors.ts`), which keeps the
  cause out of the matchable chain; tests assert `details.stopCause`
  kinds, never message text.

## Tests

- `test/helpers/inference-server.ts` (`startInferenceServer`: a script
  picks a reply per request; it records method, path, headers, raw and
  parsed body) and `inference-stream.ts` (`writeInferenceStream`, the
  native SSE per protocol).
- Byte-level body behavior (read-ahead, caps, cancel counts) is tested
  with a `highWaterMark: 0` `ReadableStream` returned through
  `ClientConfig.fetch`; `new Response(stream)` neither copies nor
  pre-reads it.
- Agent-loop tests drive `harness(script)` from
  `test/llm/agent-helpers.ts` (a scripted fake `ModelClient`: canned
  turns, errors, or functions of the request; sleeps recorded).
- `AbortSignal.timeout` timers do not keep Node alive, so a fake step
  that waits for an abort must hold a timer (the `aborted()` helper
  does); otherwise the test process just exits.
# Config, roles, and providers

Go: `internal/config`, `internal/cli/config.go`. TS: `src/config/`,
`src/cli/config.ts`. Credential lookup order is in `auth.md`.

## Loading

- Go `config.Load(path)` parses `.roast.json` as JSONC
  (`hujson.Standardize`, then `encoding/json` with
  `DisallowUnknownFields`). `version: 1` is required; a literal
  `api_key` is rejected. There are no built-in roles or models: a role
  missing from the config fails in `ResolveRole`.
- TS `jsonc.ts` parses into null-prototype objects (`newObject` /
  `setKey`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary key). `schema.ts` (zod) checks
  shape and types only, and `load.ts` keeps the validated *input*, never
  zod's output (zod's record output turns a `__proto__` key into a
  prototype). Range and cross-field checks are plain functions in
  `load.ts` so Go's check order holds.
- `config.Find(start, stop)` walks up to `stop` (the Git toplevel),
  comparing directories after `EvalSymlinks`: macOS temp dirs are
  `/var/...` while `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints
  `/private/var/...`.
- The CLI loads config only through `loadProjectConfig` (Go also
  `projectConfigPath`; TS `loadProjectConfig(explicit, ctx)`), which
  attaches the stored-credentials store. A missing config is a usage
  error naming `npx roast init`.
- Relative prompt-override paths resolve against the config file's
  directory: `config.Load` records it and callers read prompts through
  `cfg.PromptPath(...)`, never the raw `cfg.Prompts.*` string. The trust
  projection hashes the raw string, so approvals survive worktree moves.

## Settings

Only credentials come from outside the config. Every other setting is
in it (`limits`, `interactive`, `gate`, `review.snapshot`); optional
numbers are pointers so an explicit 0 differs from unset, and
`validateSettings` checks ranges. Go `cli.go` resolves them with
`flagOrConfigInt` / `flagOrConfigDuration` (flag, then config, then
default).

## Schema and starter

- `roast.schema.json` (repo root, shipped through `package.json`
  `files`) is generated: `npm run schema` renders
  `src/config/json-schema.ts` from the zod schema. Adding, removing, or
  retyping a config field changes `config.Config` and the zod schema in
  the same change, then regenerates; never edit the JSON by hand.
  `TestSchemaMatchesConfigStruct` (Go) and `test/config/schema.ts` fail
  on drift. Editor-only constraints (enum, pattern, minimum, required)
  go in the zod `.meta()`.
- The `roast init` starter (`internal/config/starter.go`) shows
  commented-out defaults that must track the defaults in
  `internal/cli/cli.go`.
- The starter places a model by its ID alone (`placeModel`): a
  `<vendor>/<model>[:<variant>]` ID goes to OpenRouter, a bare ID to the
  provider `InferFamily` names. A placeholder role follows an OpenRouter
  role onto OpenRouter; otherwise it takes the other direct provider.

## Providers

- `LookupProvider(name)` and `ProviderAt(endpoint, protocol)`
  (`internal/config/provider.go`) are the one place that says which
  endpoint, protocols, standard key variable, key page, and login method
  a provider has. Trust standardness, ambient credentials, and the
  `init` starter consult it; add a provider there, not in those callers.
  A provider's first protocol is the one starter profiles use.
- A new provider's key variable also goes in `clearProviderEnv`
  (`internal/config/credential_test.go`).
- `DefaultBaseURL(protocol)` stays separate: it is the implicit endpoint
  of a profile that omits one; OpenRouter is never one.
- A role that gets no ambient key reports any set provider key variable
  as `Withheld` (`withheldKey`), preferring the protocol's default
  provider.
- TS `provider.ts` `parseEndpoint` is the one endpoint parser (treats
  `https:///v1` and `https:v1` as an empty host, like Go); `endpoint.ts`
  and `sameEndpoint` use it.

## TS roles (`role.ts`, `credential.ts`, `provider.ts`)

- Resolution takes `env` explicitly and never reads `process.env`.
- `ResolvedRole` is frozen with a non-enumerable `apiKey` and `stored`,
  so JSON, spread, and `util.inspect` omit the secret. Change a role
  with `updateRole(role, changes)`; a `{...role}` spread drops the key.
- `StoredCredentials` lives in `credential.ts` (re-exported by
  `load.ts`).

## TS tests

- Role tests build configs with `configFrom` / `resolveFinder` and the
  in-memory `FakeStore` in `test/config/helpers.ts`.
- Configs the tests load as realistic examples go in
  `test/config/examples.ts`; the schema test validates each with ajv.
# Trust

Go: `internal/config/trust.go`, `internal/trust`, `internal/cli/trust.go`.
TS: `src/config/trust.ts`, `src/config/canonical.ts`,
`src/trust/trust.ts`, `src/cli/trust.ts`. Design: ADR-0001, ADR-0003.
How CLI tests approve a config is in `go.md`.

- `Config.TrustProjection()` is the security-relevant projection; its
  `Hash()` covers only endpoint, protocol, credential variable, and
  prompt-override paths. A new security-relevant config field goes into
  the projection, or it can change without re-approval.
- Approvals live in `<git-common-dir>/roast/trust.json`. `roast review`
  calls `requireTrust` before building scope; untrusted is a usage error
  (exit 2), never `ExitFindings`.
- TS `src/config/trust.ts` (projection, `requiresApproval`, hash v2) is
  pure. `src/config/canonical.ts` is the only JSON canonicalizer; its
  input types must be `type` aliases, not interfaces, to be assignable
  to `CanonicalValue`.
- TS `src/trust/trust.ts` takes a trailing `{signal, env}` for git.
  `src/cli/trust.ts` has the command, `writeTrustSummary` (every value
  through `textsafe.line`) and `requireTrust(dir, cfg, io)`.
  `promptNoDefault` (`[y/N]`) lives in `src/cli/terminal.ts`.
---
status: accepted
---

# Stored credentials, scoped to the project or the user

Credentials used to come only from environment variables. Every shell,
editor, and pre-commit gate that ran a review had to export a key by hand,
which is the main thing standing between `npx roast init` and a first
review. Roast now also reads a **stored credential**: a key for a built-in
provider kept in a credentials file that `npx roast login` writes.

There are two scopes. The project scope is
`<git-common-dir>/roast/credentials.json`, next to the trust record
(ADR-0001): it lives inside `.git`, so it cannot be committed, `git clone`
never copies it, and linked worktrees share it. The user scope (`login
--global`) is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/roast/credentials.json`, falling back to
`~/.config/roast/credentials.json`. Both are JSON with `version: 1` and a map
from built-in provider name to a key and when it was stored; unknown
providers and fields are rejected. Files are written atomically with mode
0600 in a 0700 directory, and on Unix a file that group or others can read is
an error telling the user to `chmod 600` it, never silently skipped.

Stored credentials rank below every environment variable, so CI and existing
setups keep working unchanged. A role's credential is the first of
`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`, the profile's `api_key_env`, the provider's standard
variable, the project-scope stored key, then the user-scope stored key. The
last three apply only when the role is on that built-in provider's endpoint
with a protocol it accepts there. A stored key is matched to a profile only
through the built-in provider registry (endpoint plus protocol), never
through anything the project config names, so a profile on a custom
endpoint, or a role an override moved off its provider's endpoint, never
receives one. The project config still never holds a credential.

## Considered options

- OS keychain (macOS Keychain, Secret Service, Windows Credential Manager):
  better at-rest protection, but needs a new dependency per platform, fails
  on headless Linux and in containers without a keyring daemon, and has no
  natural per-project scope.
- A user-only credentials file: simpler, but a project scope was asked for,
  so different projects can use different keys (for example separate
  billing) without environment juggling.
- Not storing credentials: keeps the old model, but leaves the setup problem
  this decision exists to solve.

A plain file with strict permissions needs no new dependency, works headless
and on every OS, and supports both scopes. It does not encrypt the key at
rest; anyone who can read the user's files can read it, as with the shell
profiles that held these keys before.
---
status: proposed
---

# Trust approvals hash a canonical JSON projection

Partly supersedes ADR-0001: the trust model is unchanged, but how an approval
is hashed and stored changes.

The TypeScript rewrite (ADR-0004) would otherwise have to reproduce the Go
implementation's exact serialization of the trust projection to keep
existing approvals valid. Nothing beyond `0.0.0` was published to npm, so
the only approvals in the wild belong to users of the Go binary, and asking
them to approve once more is cheap. We take the break and pick a hash that
any implementation can reproduce from a written definition.

The projection keeps the same fields as ADR-0001: per role, the endpoint,
protocol, and credential variable name, plus the raw prompt-override paths.
The approval hash is the SHA-256 of the projection serialized as RFC 8785
(JSON Canonicalization Scheme) canonical JSON, so key order and whitespace
never change the hash. `trust.json` carries `version: 2`. A version-1 file,
written by the Go implementation, is not an approval: the config is
untrusted, the message says it was approved by an older Roast and names
`npx roast trust`, and `review` exits 2 like any other untrusted config.
Approving rewrites the file as version 2.

## Considered options

- Reproduce the Go serialization byte for byte: keeps existing approvals,
  but ties the hash to an implementation detail that must be reverse
  engineered and pinned by fixtures forever.
- Accept both versions during a migration window: more code on the
  security-relevant path for a handful of users who can re-approve in one
  command.
---
status: proposed
---

# Roast is a TypeScript package that talks to providers over raw fetch

Roast was a Go binary shipped through npm as six platform packages and a
launcher. Every Roast install is already a Node project dependency, so a Go
toolchain, per-platform builds, and the launcher add release machinery
without serving users. We rewrite Roast in TypeScript as one pure-JS npm
package, `2.0.0`, with behavior parity against Go commit `cd08f10` except
for named changes: no Windows support, no `completion` command, Node
`>=22.18`, and the trust hash in ADR-0003.

Runtime libraries (`yargs`, `zod`, `jsonc-parser`,
`@substrate-system/debug`) are bundled into one esbuild ESM file, so
installing Roast adds no transitive packages to a user's project, the same
property the Go binary had. Their license texts ship in a generated
third-party notice.

Inference uses raw `fetch` with one adapter per protocol (`chat`,
`responses`, `anthropic`) and Roast's own agent loop, not a provider SDK
or an LLM framework. The Go version used the Fantasy framework but had to
wrap or override much of it: it captured raw response bytes for run
records, removed headers the SDKs read from ambient environment variables,
replaced retry and tool-call-repair behavior, sized requests for the token
ledger before sending, and reserved a wrap-up turn. Official SDKs would need
the same overrides on two codebases; owning the wire layer puts all of it in
one place.

## Considered options

- Keep Go: no rewrite cost, but keeps the multi-package release and a second
  toolchain for a tool whose users all run Node.
- Official `openai` and `@anthropic-ai/sdk` SDKs: typed streaming for free,
  but both read ambient configuration and run their own retries, which Roast
  must defeat to keep its credential and capture guarantees.
- Vercel AI SDK: closest to Fantasy, but it hides the request bytes and the
  retry policy that the ledger and run records depend on.
